<TopicPage slug="subjects/cognitive-science/foundational/what-is-cognitive-science">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Cognitive Science, and it assumes no prior study of
    psychology, neuroscience or philosophy — only curiosity about your own mind and patience
    with a few unfamiliar terms, each defined where it first appears. Everything else is built
    here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the field's founding wager — that minds process information — and explain what the wager promises and what it leaves open;</li>
    <li>outline the mind–body problem, and describe the field's working answers (<em>dualism</em>, <em>physicalism</em>, <em>functionalism</em>);</li>
    <li>explain what a mental <em>representation</em> is, and cite three classic lines of evidence that minds build them;</li>
    <li>use David Marr's three levels of explanation to say why psychology, neuroscience and computation do not compete;</li>
    <li>explain why a running model counts as a testable theory of mind;</li>
    <li>recount how the field escaped behaviourism's fifty-year ban on the mind, and name the landmarks of 1956.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit governs everything: when an explanation of the mind sounds like magic, ask at
    which <em>level</em> it is pitched. Most famous quarrels about mind dissolve into level
    confusions once parsed — and learning to parse them is most of the discipline.
  </p>

  <h2>The wager: a mind can be studied like a system</h2>
  <p>
    Cognitive science is built on a single, enormous wager: that minds are systems which
    process information, and can therefore be studied the way other complex systems are —
    with models, measurements and experiments. When you read this sentence, light patterns on
    your retina become letters, letters become words, words become meaning; when you later
    recall it, something in your brain is retrieved, recombined and expressed. The wager says
    those events have <em>structure</em> — describable, modelled, tested — and that we do not
    have to choose between ignoring the mind's mystery and declaring it unknowable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The wager is old enough to have ancestors. In the eighteenth century, David Hume guessed
    that inner representations of knowledge are formed according to particular rules — and
    that their transformations take <em>effort and time</em>. If the mind runs on operations,
    operations leave traces in how long they take. In the nineteenth century, Wilhelm Wundt
    and Franciscus Donders turned that guess into measurement, timing how long simple mental
    responses take — an approach whose interpretation, decades later, would help found
    cognitive psychology.
  </p>
  <p>
    No single discipline could cash the wager alone. Psychology measures what minds do
    (behaviour, reaction times, errors) but not how; neuroscience measures brains but drowns
    in detail without questions posed from above; linguistics reveals the structure of the
    mind's most characteristic product; philosophy keeps the concepts honest; and computer
    science builds the models that turn hunches into running, failing, testable theories. The
    field is the triangulation — literally the name for the method: fix the unknown mind by
    measuring it from several disciplinary angles at once, and let the lines cross.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the founding wager, unpacked" widget="equationStep" config={cogsciCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are load-bearing, in order. The wager
    (step 1) only pays off if the levels are kept distinct (step 2), which is why the field
    must be interdisciplinary (step 3); models are how the wager becomes falsifiable (step
    4); and convergence — agreement from independent angles — is what counts as progress
    (step 5). Each section below takes one step and grounds it in classic experiments.
  </p>

  <h2>The mind–body problem</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest obstacle is conceptual: what <em>is</em> the relation between mental things
    (thoughts, feelings, that pinkness of the evening sky) and physical things (neurons,
    voltages, chemistry)? René Descartes' seventeenth-century answer — two distinct
    substances, mind and matter, interacting in the pineal gland — made the problem sharp and
    bequeathed <em>dualism</em>, which still shapes every everyday theory of mind. The modern
    problem survives Descartes in better form: if the mental <em>is</em> the physical (as
    almost all scientists assume — <em>physicalism</em>), why does it feel like anything at
    all, and how do the qualitative feels (<em>qualia</em>) arise from membrane voltages?
    David Chalmers' framing (1995) called the first questions — what does the mind
    <em>do</em>? — the "easy" problems, and this one — why is there experience? — the
    <em>hard problem</em>. The naming was rhetorical, but it drew the field's honest boundary:
    work on the easy problems is not evasion of the hard one, and progress on memory,
    perception and language proceeds whether or not anyone has explained why it feels like
    something.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cognitive science's characteristic move is <em>functionalism</em>: define mental states by
    what they <em>do</em> — their causes and effects, their role in the system — rather than
    by what they are made of. A memory is whatever plays memory's functional role; in
    principle, the same role could be played by neurons, silicon or (the thought experiment)
    water pipes. Functionalism is why the field can take computers seriously as models of mind
    without claiming brains are computers, and it is the philosophical licence for the whole
    enterprise: if mind is organisation rather than substance, then minds can be studied,
    built and compared across substrates. Alan Turing supplied the operational spirit in 1950
    — replace "can machines think?" with a test you can actually administer (can its behaviour
    be told apart from a human's?) — and John Searle's Chinese Room argument (1980) supplied
    the standing objection: symbol-manipulation that passes tests might still lack
    understanding. The argument between them, refined but unresolved, is cognitive science's
    oldest running experiment.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="walk the three classic answers to the mind–body problem" widget="branch" config={cgsFunctionBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Turing test is a proof — a machine that passes it has
  been shown to think. It is a behavioural criterion, not a metaphysical one: passing
  establishes that the responses are indistinguishable from a human's, and leaves exactly the
  question Searle's Chinese Room presses — whether fluent symbol-manipulation amounts to
  understanding. The field's honest use of the test is as a marker of what behaviour can
  settle, which is why the argument it started has run for decades rather than being closed
  by any passing score.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — what the Turing test settles" widget="judge" config={cgsTuringJudge} />

  <h2>Representation and computation</h2>
  <p>
    The positive theory of the wager has two clauses. Minds use <em>representations</em>:
    inner states that stand for things — the cat in your absence, the next word in this
    sentence, the location of your keys. And minds <em>compute</em> over those
    representations: they transform them according to rules or regularities — perceiving is
    transforming light into objects, remembering is encoding, storing and reconstructing,
    reasoning is chaining representations toward conclusions. The claim is not metaphor but
    mechanism: if you can specify the representations and the transformations precisely enough
    to build one, the theory becomes testable by running it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The evidence that minds really build inner representations comes from experiments that
    measure their shape indirectly, and it arrives in a graduated series. First, timing:
    nineteenth-century reaction-time work (Donders, Wundt) showed that mental operations take
    measurable, orderly amounts of time — exactly what Hume had guessed. Next, animals: in
    the 1920s, Wolfgang Köhler's chimpanzees, faced with a banana hung too high to reach and
    boxes scattered on the floor, learned to stack the boxes and climb — behaviour that
    suggests they could <em>visualise</em> the result before acting, an early demonstration
    of insight rather than mere trial and error. Maze-running rats in the same era sharpened
    the point: rats allowed to explore a maze with no food reward caught up almost at once
    once food appeared, as if they had been learning the layout all along. Edward Tolman
    argued the rats had formed a <strong>cognitive map</strong> — a mental representation
    storing where things are — not a chained series of left-right turns.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="fill the blanks — what the latencies revealed" widget="cloze" config={cgsMapCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a rat that learns a maze has simply memorised a sequence
  of turns, like a robot on a track. It seems the leaner explanation, and for decades
  behaviourism treated maze-running that way. But the latencies say otherwise: rats rewarded
  only late in training improve almost immediately, and rats that have watched a shorter
  route take it when their usual path is blocked. Tolman's cognitive map — an inner
  representation of the layout — explains both, which is why the map idea matters: it was
  the first proof that representations are not a human luxury.</p>
  <p>
    The human classic is mental rotation. Roger Shepard and Jacqueline Metzler (1971) showed
    people drawings of paired 3-D shapes — same object or mirror image? — rotated by various
    angles, and measured decision times. The result was a straight line: response time rose
    in lockstep with the angular difference, at roughly a second for every additional 60
    degrees of turn, as if participants were rotating one image at a constant speed and
    comparing when the angles matched. Whatever a mental
    image is, it behaves like a spatial representation you operate on — a finding that
    launched a decade-long debate about whether imagery is truly picture-like, and a template
    for how the field argues: propose an inner representation, predict what operations on it
    should cost, and time the mind against the prediction.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the cost of one more turn" widget="estimate" config={cgsRotateEst} />
  <p>
    The twentieth century supplied the formal machinery. Turing's 1936 analysis defined
    computation itself — what any mechanical procedure can, in principle, do — and showed a
    single machine (the universal one) could perform any of it. Allen Newell and Herbert
    Simon drew the moral for minds in their <em>physical symbol system hypothesis</em> (1976):
    intelligence is, at root, the manipulation of symbols, in brains as in programs — and
    their theorem-proving and problem-solving programs were the first serious models of higher
    thought. The <em>connectionist</em> counter-movement (Rumelhart and McClelland's parallel
    distributed processing, 1986) objected that the brain's units are not symbols but simple
    neurons whose intelligence emerges from networks learning their own weights — the
    tradition that, scaled up decades later, became deep learning. Bayesian modelling offered
    a third grammar: the mind as an inference engine, updating prior beliefs with evidence the
    way statistics prescribes. And modern artificial-neuron networks have become part of the
    evidence base itself: when a network trained on memory-like tasks reproduces the results
    of real memory experiments, the psychological model it embodies gains support — and the
    large language models add an unexpected epilogue, artefacts trained on nothing but text
    that now serve as subjects of psychological study in their own right.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the wager has a positive theory — minds represent, and minds
    compute over representations — plus a graduated evidence file: timed reactions, rats with
    cognitive maps, and human mental rotation. What remains is to say why brain-talk and
    mind-talk never actually disagree.
  </p>

  <h2>Levels of explanation</h2>
  <p>
    Why did you remember your password? Psychologically: it is in long-term memory, cued by
    the login screen's context. Biologically: synapses in your hippocampus strengthened
    through consolidation, a process that ran last night in sleep. Computationally: a
    retrieval process matched a key against stored entries and returned the value. Three
    descriptions, zero contradictions — and David Marr's great methodological insight (1982)
    was that this is structural, not coincidental. Any information-processing system can be
    explained at three levels: the <em>computational</em> (what problem is it solving, and
    why?), the <em>algorithmic</em> (what representations and steps does it use?), and the
    <em>implementational</em> (what physical machinery runs those steps?). Biology lives at
    the bottom level, psychology mostly at the middle, the theory of the task at the top.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each story of one memory with its question" widget="match" config={cgsMarrMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the levels compete — "a real explanation" must be neural,
  and psychological talk is loose talk. The levels are related like software, program and
  hardware: knowing the transistors does not give you the spreadsheet, and knowing the
  spreadsheet does not give you the transistors — but both, at their own level, are exact.
  The field's classic errors are level-crossings: "a memory is a synapse" (implementation
  mistaken for algorithm) or "the visual system computes because it is a computer program"
  (algorithm mistaken for implementation). Keeping the levels straight is the discipline's
  table manners, and most famous quarrels about mind dissolve into level confusions once
  parsed.</p>

  <h2>Models that run</h2>
  <p>
    What separates cognitive science from armchair philosophy of mind is executable theory: a
    model is a hypothesis written precisely enough to <em>fail</em>. The symbolic tradition
    built production systems — rules over symbols — that solved problems the way Newell and
    Simon argued humans do, even reproducing the errors; their General Problem Solver was
    evaluated against human protocols line by line. Connectionist networks showed that
    structured behaviour can be <em>learned</em> rather than programmed: trained on
    past-tense verbs or letter recognition, they made (tellingly) the same mistakes children
    make. Bayesian models formalised perception and reasoning as optimal inference under
    uncertainty, predicting not just behaviour but its rational structure. Each grammar
    captured something and missed something, and the argument between them — which
    representations, what learning, how much is innate — is the field's productive engine.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each result under the grammar that produced it" widget="sort-buckets" config={cgsGrammarSort} />
  <p>
    The method has a characteristic rhythm, worth watching once. A behavioural experiment
    finds a regularity (say, people systematically judge some risks as more likely than their
    frequencies warrant). A modeller asks what representation and process would
    <em>produce</em> that regularity, and builds one — then tests it against the data it was
    built on, and against new data it has never seen. Neuroscience checks whether the brain
    plausibly implements such a process — today with tools like EEG and functional MRI;
    comparative work checks whether other species share it; development checks how it comes
    to be. If the model reproduces the behaviour for the right reasons, the theory has
    survived one more test — and if it fails, the failure is usually more informative than
    the success.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the founding wager in one sentence, and name one thing it deliberately leaves open.</li>
    <li>What is a cognitive map, and whose experiments argued for it?</li>
    <li>A friend says memory is "just synapses". Which of Marr's levels are being confused, and what would a better answer look like?</li>
    <li>Why does a running model count as more than an analogy?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Minds are systems that process information —
    representations transformed by regular operations — and so can be studied with models,
    measurements and experiments. It leaves open the hard problem: why any of it is
    accompanied by experience. (2) A mental representation of an environment's layout —
    where things are relative to each other. Edward Tolman argued for it from rats that
    apparently learned mazes without reward and took novel shortcuts. (3) It collapses the
    algorithmic level into the implementational one — "memory is a synapse" mistakes the
    hardware for the program. Better: the <em>algorithm</em> is what stores and retrieves,
    the synapse is one way brains implement it, and the two descriptions can both be exact.
    (4) Because it is precise enough to fail: it makes predictions about behaviour (and even
    its errors) that can be checked against data, rather than gesturing at a mechanism.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The founding wager: minds process information, so perceiving, remembering and reasoning can be studied scientifically.</li>
    <li>Dualism, physicalism and functionalism are the classic answers to the mind–body problem; the "hard problem" of experience is the honest leftover.</li>
    <li>Minds build representations — evidenced by timed reactions, Köhler's insight-solving chimpanzees, Tolman's cognitive maps in rats, and mental rotation's linear response times.</li>
    <li>Minds compute over representations; the field's three grammars of the computation are symbolic, connectionist and Bayesian.</li>
    <li>Marr's levels — computational, algorithmic, implementational — explain why psychology, neuroscience and computation complement rather than compete.</li>
    <li>A model is a theory you can run: it counts because it can fail, and its failures teach.</li>
    <li>Triangulation is the method: no single discipline could cash the wager alone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lessons put the wager to work on the mind's individual faculties: perception as
    inference, attention as a bottleneck, memory's systems, language and reasoning. Each
    chapter uses the same toolkit you have just assembled — a behavioural regularity, a
    representational hypothesis, a model, and a level-check against the brain — so the
    discipline you practised here is the one the whole subject runs on.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The founding moment is worth knowing precisely, because the field's shape follows it —
    and so is the century of opposition before it. For roughly fifty years, <em>behaviourism</em>
    ruled psychology: John Watson and B. F. Skinner held that mental operations — attention,
    memory, thinking — are hypothetical constructs that cannot be observed or proven, so
    science should study only observable behaviour in response to stimuli. Talk of the mind
    was banished, and investigation of mental events was largely abandoned. In hindsight the
    standard story is a little too tidy — Skinner spoke of "private events" rather than
    denying inner life, and his methods generated durable practical results — but the ban was
    real, and the 1950s lifted it. In 1956, within months: the Dartmouth workshop coined
    "artificial intelligence"; George Miller published the magic number seven (a manifesto
    for mental processes as measurable quantities); and Noam Chomsky's work on formal
    language began the assault on behaviourism, completed by his 1959 review of Skinner —
    the argument that children produce sentences they have never heard cannot be explained by
    stimulus and response, and demands internal structure. By the late 1970s the convergence
    had institutions: the Cognitive Science journal (1977) and the Cognitive Science Society
    (1979). "The cognitive revolution" was thus less a discovery than a permission slip: to
    talk about representations again, with methods.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="put the revolution's landmarks in order" widget="order" config={cgs1956Order} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Miller's "magic number seven" means working memory holds
  seven items. The 1956 paper argued for seven plus or minus two <em>chunks</em> — meaningful
  groupings, not raw items — and later estimates put the raw capacity nearer four. The chunk
  is the real discovery: by packing letters into acronyms and digits into dates, a
  fixed-capacity system carries unbounded content, which is why expert memory (chess masters,
  musicians) looks like a bigger store but is really better compression.</p>
  <p>
    The core curriculum circles a fixed set of puzzles, each now a field: how perception
    builds a stable world from ambiguous, noisy input (the classic demonstration being that
    vision is inference, not photography); why attention is a bottleneck and what it selects;
    memory's systems (working versus long-term, and the multiple systems revealed by patients
    like H.M., who could learn skills he could not remember learning); how children come to
    reason about possibilities at all — Jean Piaget's pendulum problem, in which a young
    person must work out what makes a pendulum swing fast by varying one factor while
    mentally holding the others constant, is the classic picture of that step — and how
    people actually reason, including why the heuristics that serve us well also produce the
    systematic biases catalogued by the judgment-and-decision-making tradition. Behind the
    puzzles runs the oldest debate of all, nature versus nurture — how much structure is
    innate and how much learned — which fields from Chomsky's linguistics to sociobiology's
    evolutionary accounts of behaviour keep re-arguing with new evidence. The open frontiers
    are the hard ones: the neuroscience of consciousness (global workspace and
    integrated-information theories now argue in adversarial collaborations), embodied
    cognition's challenge to the head-bound computer metaphor, and the question the large
    language models force — which parts of mind ride on biology, and which are just
    information processing that any substrate will do. The wager is still open; the odds have
    never looked more interesting.
  </p>

